/**
 * Answer bank for followup questions — Spring Data JPA / Hibernate category.
 * Keyed by the exact followup `text` so answers merge into the FollowUp
 * objects at runtime without touching questions.ts.
 *
 * Style (per answer_rules.md):
 *  - Length follows the question — no sentence quota, no padding
 *  - Bold the terms that actually matter
 *  - No GPT fluff, no "In conclusion", active voice, contractions
 *  - BAD/GOOD code only where it proves understanding
 */
export const followupAnswersJpa: Record<string, string> = {

  // ===================== Q84: Spring Data JPA basics =====================
  "How do repository interfaces get implemented at runtime?":
    "Spring Data scans for interfaces extending `Repository` at application startup — `@EnableJpaRepositories` triggers the scan, and Spring Boot switches it on for you. For each interface it creates a **proxy** backed by `SimpleJpaRepository`, which is where the real `save` and `findById` implementations actually live. The proxy intercepts every call and routes it to the right strategy: the derived-name parser, the `@Query` executor, or a base `SimpleJpaRepository` method. You never write an implementation class — the proxy *is* the implementation, which is why there's no `UserRepositoryImpl` anywhere in the codebase. It also means a typo in a derived method name fails at **startup**, not on the first request.",

  "What is the difference between Spring Data JPA and JDBC Template?":
    "**`JdbcTemplate`** is a thin wrapper over raw JDBC — you write the SQL, you map the `ResultSet` rows to objects, and nothing happens that you didn't ask for. **Spring Data JPA** sits on top of Hibernate and gives you an entity model instead: relationships, dirty checking, caching, and no SQL at all for ordinary CRUD. Reach for `JdbcTemplate` when you want full control of the SQL — bulk operations, or a reporting query with three joins and a `GROUP BY`. Use Spring Data JPA when the domain is genuinely object-relational and you're mostly doing CRUD on it. Mixing both in one app is normal, and plenty of production codebases do exactly that: JPA for the entities, `JdbcTemplate` for the awkward read queries.",

  // ===================== Q85: JPA vs Hibernate vs Spring Data JPA =====================
  "Is Hibernate a JPA implementation or a separate API?":
    "Hibernate is **both**. It's a full ORM with its own API — `Session`, `Criteria`, `HQL` — and that API predates JPA by years. It also **implements** the JPA standard, so the same library serves `EntityManager`, `JPQL` and `@Entity`. You can see both at once in code: `entityManager.unwrap(Session.class)` hands you the Hibernate `Session` behind the JPA interface you were holding. The spec itself lives in `jakarta.persistence`, or `javax.persistence` if you're on anything before Boot 3. And you get Hibernate without asking — `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` pulls it in as the default provider.",

  "Can you use Hibernate features that are not in the JPA standard?":
    "Yes, and you probably already are. `@BatchSize`, `@CreationTimestamp` and `@UpdateTimestamp`, `@NaturalId`, `@Filter` — none of those are in the JPA spec, they're all Hibernate extensions. The trade-off is **vendor lock-in**: lean on them and you can't swap to EclipseLink without rewriting those parts. In practice almost no production app ever makes that switch, so the risk you're insuring against mostly doesn't happen. If you need a Hibernate feature, use it. Don't write 200 lines of workaround to stay \"pure JPA\" for a portability you'll never exercise.",

  // ===================== Q86: Repository hierarchy =====================
  "What is the difference between `findById` and `getReferenceById`?":
    "`findById` runs a `SELECT` immediately and hands you the entity, or an empty `Optional`. `getReferenceById` runs **no query at all** — it returns a lazy proxy with only the ID populated, and the SELECT fires the first time anything touches another field.\n\nThe reason to care is setting a foreign key. Creating an `OrderItem` that belongs to `Order` 42, you don't need the order's data, only its ID in the FK column — `getReferenceById(42)` gives Hibernate exactly that and saves you a read.\n\nThe catch is where a missing row surfaces. `findById` hands back an empty `Optional` you deal with on the spot. `getReferenceById` returns a proxy regardless, then throws **`EntityNotFoundException`** whenever something first touches it — possibly far outside the method that created it, or after the session closed, where you get `LazyInitializationException` instead. It's the Boot 3 replacement for the deprecated `getOne` and `getById`.",

  "Should you expose delete-all methods on production repositories?":
    "No. Both of them wipe an entire table with no filter and no confirmation, and they arrive for free the moment anyone injects the repository. It's worth knowing they don't behave the same way either. `deleteAll()` loads the rows and fires one `DELETE` per row, so cascades and lifecycle callbacks still run. `deleteAllInBatch()` issues a single `DELETE FROM users` and skips all of that — faster, and the more destructive of the two. The fix is to **narrow the repository**: extend `Repository<T, ID>` directly and declare only the methods that entity actually needs, so `deleteAll` never exists on it. Do that at the repository level rather than trusting the service layer to be careful, because the method is public API as soon as it's on the interface.",

  // ===================== Q87: @Query =====================
  "What is the difference between JPQL and native SQL in `@Query`?":
    "**JPQL** runs against your entity model, not your tables. `SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :email` names the `User` class and its Java fields, and Hibernate translates it to real SQL at runtime — so the same query works on Postgres and MySQL. **Native SQL** with `nativeQuery = true` is the raw thing: `SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?1`, tied to your actual schema and broken the day someone renames a column. There's a subtler catch with native queries too. They go around Hibernate's entity awareness, so a native `UPDATE` doesn't update entities already loaded in the persistence context, and a native `SELECT` returning columns that don't map to an entity needs a projection interface or an explicit `resultSetMapping`. Use JPQL by default. Drop to native only for things JPQL genuinely can't say — window functions, `RETURNING`, or a vendor-specific hint.",

  "How do you use named parameters vs positional parameters?":
    "**Named parameters** are `:email` in the query, matched to a method argument tagged `@Param(\"email\")`. **Positional parameters** are `?1` and `?2`, matched purely by argument order — `WHERE u.email = ?1 AND u.status = ?2`. Always prefer named, and the reason is sharper than readability. Swap the order of two same-typed arguments in the method signature and positional binding doesn't error at all — the query just runs with the values in the wrong slots, and you find out from a support ticket. Named parameters break loudly instead, or don't break at all. Positional is tolerable for a single-parameter query and a genuine maintenance trap past three.",

  "What do you have to get right when running a bulk `@Modifying` update?":
    "`@Modifying` tells Spring Data to call `executeUpdate()` instead of `getResultList()`, and the query needs a transaction around it or you get `TransactionRequiredException`. Put that `@Transactional` on the **service** method that calls the repository, since the unit of work belongs there. Worth noting JPQL only has `UPDATE` and `DELETE` — there's no portable JPQL `INSERT`, so an insert has to be a native query. The part that actually catches people is the first-level cache. A bulk `@Modifying` update goes straight to the database and never touches entities already loaded in the persistence context, so reading them back in the same transaction returns the **old** values. `@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true)` clears the context after the update so the next read goes to the DB. Pair it with `flushAutomatically = true` if you have pending changes that need to land *before* the bulk statement runs, or the update will overwrite them.",

  // ===================== Q88: Derived queries vs @Query =====================
  "When does a derived method name become ambiguous?":
    "Spring resolves each segment against your entity **greedily, longest property name first**, and that's where it picks the wrong thing. If `Employee` has both a `departmentName` field and a `department` association, `findByDepartmentName` silently binds to the flat field rather than `department.name`. You disambiguate with an underscore — `findByDepartment_Name` forces the traversal.\n\nWhen nothing matches at all you get a `PropertyReferenceException` at startup. Ugly to read, but far better than a wrong query discovered at runtime.\n\nThe readability limit usually arrives before the ambiguity one. `findByStatusAndCreatedAtAfterAndDepartmentNameOrderByCreatedAtDesc` is a real method somebody wrote — if you can't say the name aloud without losing your place, it belongs in `@Query`.",

  "How does Spring parse method names like `findByEmailAndStatus`?":
    "Spring Data reads the name left to right. First it strips the action prefix — `findBy`, `existsBy`, `countBy`, `deleteBy` — then tokenizes what's left on **camelCase boundaries** and matches each token against your entity's properties. So `findByEmailAndStatus` splits into `email` and `status`, resolves both against `User`, and builds `WHERE email = ? AND status = ?`. Words like `And`, `Or`, `Between`, `LessThan`, `IgnoreCase` and `OrderBy` are keywords that shape the predicate or the sort rather than naming a field. The important part is when this happens. Parsing runs **at startup**, so a property that doesn't exist stops the application from booting. That's the behaviour you want — the alternative is discovering a silently wrong query in production.",

  "Can derived queries support pagination and sorting?":
    "Yes — add a `Pageable` or a `Sort` as the **last argument** and Spring Data does the rest. `Page<User> findByStatus(String status, Pageable pageable)` gives you the content plus `totalElements` and page metadata. The return type is the choice that matters. `Page<T>` fires a **second `COUNT` query** to work out the total, and on a large table that count is often the expensive half of the request. `Slice<T>` skips it and just checks whether one more row exists, which is all an infinite-scroll UI actually needs. Plain `List<T>` gives you the page with no count at all. It works on `@Query` methods too, where Spring derives the count query for you — but for anything with `DISTINCT` or a join you should supply `countQuery` explicitly, or the total comes back wrong.",

  // ===================== Q89: N+1 problem =====================
  "How would you detect N+1 in logs or with a tool?":
    `The fastest check is to turn the SQL on — **\`spring.jpa.show-sql=true\`** plus **\`logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG\`** — and count. Load 20 orders, see 21 statements scroll past, that's N+1. Counting log lines by eye gets old quickly, so **\`spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true\`** is the better switch: Hibernate then prints a \`Session Metrics\` summary with the statement count per session. In a running system you want it in the query layer rather than your eyes — **p6spy** wraps the JDBC driver and logs every statement, and Hibernate can flag slow ones itself via \`hibernate.session.events.log.LOG_QUERIES_SLOWER_THAN_MS\`. Any APM that traces JDBC spans will show the same fan-out as a row of identical queries. The real fix is to stop finding it manually — **assert the query count in an integration test** and N+1 can never come back silently.

\`\`\`java
// Fail the build if a repository call regresses into N+1
var stats = entityManagerFactory.unwrap(SessionFactory.class).getStatistics();
stats.clear();

List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAllWithItems(); // should be ONE query

assertThat(orders).hasSize(50);
assertThat(stats.getPrepareStatementCount()).isEqualTo(1); // 51 => N+1 is back
\`\`\`

The point: don't discover N+1 in prod — it's a cliff, not a slope. It looks fine on 20 dev rows and melts the DB on 20,000 prod rows.`,

  "`JOIN FETCH`, `@EntityGraph`, `@BatchSize` — when does each one break?":
    "**`JOIN FETCH`** pulls the association in one query — `SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.id = :id`. **`@EntityGraph`** does the same thing declaratively on the repository method, so you don't have to write the query at all — `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\"items\"})`. **`@BatchSize(size = 25)`** takes a different route: it still loads lazily, but groups the loads into `WHERE id IN (1,2,...,25)` batches instead of one SELECT per row. So `JOIN FETCH` is the strongest at exactly 1 query, `@EntityGraph` is the cleanest to reuse, and `@BatchSize` is the cheap global fix you can apply without touching a single query. Two traps come up here. You can only `JOIN FETCH` **one** collection per query — fetch two `List` associations at once and you get `MultipleBagFetchException`. Fetch one and let `@BatchSize` handle the other, or change the `List` to a `Set`. The second: `JOIN FETCH` combined with `Pageable` makes Hibernate paginate **in memory**, logging `HHH000104` while it loads the entire result set. When you need paging and fetching together, use `@EntityGraph` over a two-step ID query.",

  "When is DTO projection a better fix than eager fetching?":
    "When the endpoint is **read-only** and needs a handful of fields — names and emails to fill a dropdown, say — pulling whole entities and their associations is wasted work. A projection makes Hibernate `SELECT` only the columns you asked for, which beats joining an entire object graph you're about to throw away. Two shapes exist and they behave differently. An **interface projection**, `interface UserSummary { String getName(); String getEmail(); }`, works straight off a derived method — Spring implements it at runtime. A **record or class** needs a constructor expression in the JPQL: `SELECT new com.app.UserSummary(u.name, u.email) FROM User u`. Forgetting that is where most people get stuck. The catch worth being honest about: a projection isn't a managed entity, so there's no dirty checking and nothing to save. That's not a bonus, it's the reason projections are read-only — if you need to modify and persist the thing, load the real entity.",

  // ===================== Q90: FetchType =====================
  "Why is EAGER the harder default to live with?":
    "The singular sides, `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToOne`, default to **EAGER**. The collection sides, `@OneToMany` and `@ManyToMany`, default to **LAZY**. The reasoning behind the spec is easy enough to follow — one foreign key is one extra join and looks cheap, while a collection could be ten thousand rows and eager-loading that is indefensible. The problem is that \"one extra join\" compounds. Three `@ManyToOne` fields on an entity, each pointing at something with its own `@ManyToOne`, and a single `findById` turns into a seven-table join you never asked for. What really settles it is that the two defaults aren't equally reversible. **LAZY is opt-out per query** — you write a `JOIN FETCH` or an `@EntityGraph` on the one query that needs the data. **EAGER isn't opt-in-able**: once it's on the mapping, every query everywhere drags that association along, and no query-level hint makes it lazy again. So you set LAZY on the mapping and fetch eagerly where it's needed, never the reverse.",

  "What is `LazyInitializationException`, and when does it occur?":
    "It fires when you touch a **lazy association after the Hibernate session has closed**. The proxy in that field needs an open session to run its `SELECT`, and once the transaction ends there's nothing left to run it on. The classic shape is a service that returns an entity with a `LAZY` collection, the transaction commits on the way out, and then something calls `order.getItems()` and it blows up. Where you actually hit it is worth knowing, because Spring Boot leaves **OSIV on by default** and that keeps the session open for the whole request. So it usually doesn't happen in a controller — it shows up in tests, in `@Async` or scheduled code, in a `CompletableFuture`, or after you've handed the entity to something outside the request thread. The fix is to load what you need while the transaction is still open, with `JOIN FETCH` or `@EntityGraph`, or to map to a DTO inside the service so nothing lazy ever escapes.",

  "How do Open Session In View (OSIV) settings affect lazy loading?":
    "**OSIV** holds the Hibernate session open for the entire HTTP request, from the filter chain through to rendering, and `spring.jpa.open-in-view=true` is Spring Boot's **default**. The upside is that lazy collections just resolve in a controller or a template and you never see `LazyInitializationException`. The cost is where those queries end up running. The session is still open while Jackson walks your entity to build the JSON, so **Hibernate is firing SELECTs during serialization** — N+1 generated by a response writer, in code you never wrote and can't see in the service. Boot actually warns you about this at startup, in a message almost everyone scrolls past. Set `open-in-view=false` and be explicit instead: fetch what the endpoint needs inside the service, return a DTO, and the query count becomes something you decide rather than something Jackson discovers.",

  // ===================== Q91: Entity relationships =====================
  "How do you keep both sides of a bidirectional relationship in sync?":
    `The **owning side** is the one holding the foreign key column, and it's the side **without** \`mappedBy\`. The **inverse side** carries \`mappedBy = "fieldName"\`, which tells Hibernate the other end manages this link so don't write an FK here.

The consequence people miss is that \`mappedBy\` makes the inverse side **read-only for persistence**. Hibernate reads the owning side and nothing else when it decides what SQL to emit — changes on the inverse side aren't merged in, they're ignored outright. Add an \`OrderItem\` to \`order.getItems()\`, skip \`item.setOrder(order)\`, and the insert runs with a null FK.

"Always set both sides" is true but unenforceable, so real codebases put a helper on the parent and make it impossible to get wrong:

\`\`\`java
// Order.java — one call, both sides, no way to forget
public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
    items.add(item);
    item.setOrder(this);   // the owning side — this is what writes the FK
}

public void removeItem(OrderItem item) {
    items.remove(item);
    item.setOrder(null);
}
\`\`\`

Then callers use \`order.addItem(item)\` and never touch either collection directly.`,

  "How do you model a many-to-many that needs extra columns on the join?":
    "A plain `@ManyToMany` gives you a hidden join table with exactly two FK columns and nowhere to put anything else — no `enrolledAt`, no `role`, no `status`. The moment you need one of those, you promote the join table to a real entity. Create `StudentCourse` with its own `@Id` and the extra columns on it, give it a `@ManyToOne` to `Student` and a `@ManyToOne` to `Course`, and point a `@OneToMany` back from each parent. You've traded the convenience of `@ManyToMany` for full control, and you can now query the join directly — \"who enrolled last week\" becomes an ordinary repository method. Most `@ManyToMany` relationships end up here eventually, because the business asks for a date or a status on the link sooner or later. The ones that survive as-is are the genuinely attribute-free joins, like tags on an article.",

  "What cascade types are commonly used, and when is `CascadeType.ALL` dangerous?":
    "**`PERSIST`** saves the children when you save the parent, **`MERGE`** propagates a merge the same way, and **`REMOVE`** deletes the children when the parent is deleted. Those three cover almost everything you'll actually want. **`CascadeType.ALL`** is the dangerous one because it quietly includes `REMOVE`. Put `ALL` on a `@ManyToMany` and deleting one `Course` cascades into the join rows and then into `Student` entities that other courses still reference — you set out to delete a course and took students with it. Worth separating out one thing people conflate: **`orphanRemoval = true` is not a cascade type**. `REMOVE` deletes children when the parent is deleted, while `orphanRemoval` deletes a child the moment it leaves the parent's collection, parent still very much alive. So `order.getItems().remove(item)` issues a `DELETE` under `orphanRemoval` and does nothing under `REMOVE` alone. Use `orphanRemoval` where the child genuinely can't exist without the parent, name your cascades explicitly rather than reaching for `ALL`, and never cascade `REMOVE` across a `@ManyToMany`.",

  // ===================== Q92: Caching =====================
  "A batch job loads 100,000 rows in one transaction and runs out of memory. Why?":
    "Because the **first-level cache is unbounded** and it's holding every one of them. Every entity you load stays in the persistence context until the transaction ends, so one long transaction accumulates the whole result set in memory.\n\nIt's the Hibernate session cache: always on, scoped to one session — one `@Transactional` boundary in Spring — and you can't disable it or size it. It's part of the `EntityManager` contract, not a feature you opt into.\n\nThe fix is `entityManager.clear()` every few hundred rows, or transactions short enough that it never becomes a question. The other consequence of the design is identity: it returns the **same object instance** for the same ID, so `a == b` is actually true for two lookups of `User` 1 in one transaction, and a change made through one reference is visible through the other.",

  "How do you enable and configure the second-level cache?":
    "Three steps. Add a provider — `hibernate-jcache` with Ehcache is the usual pick — then set `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache=true` and point `hibernate.cache.region.factory_class` at it. The third step is the one people forget: annotate each entity with **`@Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)`**. Turn L2 on and skip the annotation and absolutely nothing gets cached — it's opt-in per entity, and there's no warning to tell you. Note that `@Cache` is `org.hibernate.annotations.Cache`, a Hibernate annotation rather than a JPA one, so it's Hibernate you're configuring here, not the spec. Pick the strategy by how the data changes. `READ_ONLY` for reference data that never updates — currencies, country codes. `READ_WRITE` for mutable entities, where Hibernate uses soft locks to keep the cache consistent with the transaction. `NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE` is cheaper because it skips that locking, at the price of a brief window where two nodes can read different values — fine for a product description, not for a balance.",

  "What is query cache, and when is it useful?":
    "The **query cache** keys on the query string plus its parameters, and stores the **list of entity IDs** the query returned — not the rows themselves. On a repeat call with identical parameters it hands back those IDs and Hibernate resolves each one through the L2 cache. That mechanism carries a trap directly: with the query cache on and **L2 off**, every cached ID turns into its own `SELECT`, so you've converted one query into N and made things slower. The two go together or not at all. It pays off for expensive read-mostly queries called repeatedly with the same arguments — `findAll()` over a small reference table is the textbook case. It's worthless when the parameters differ every call, since no key ever repeats, and it actively hurts on volatile tables because **any** write to a table invalidates every cached query touching it. Enable it per query with `@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = \"org.hibernate.cacheable\", value = \"true\"))`, never globally.",

  // ===================== Q93: save / saveAndFlush / persist =====================
  "Why would `save()` fire a SELECT before it inserts?":
    "Because it took the `merge()` path. `save()` decides between insert and update by asking whether the entity is **new**, and the check is the `@Id` field — `null`, or `0` for a primitive, means new, so it calls `persist()`. A `@Version` field is consulted too if there is one. Any other ID and it calls `merge()`, which issues a `SELECT` to load the current row before the `UPDATE`.\n\nOn a genuinely detached entity that's correct, just an extra query most people don't expect to be paying for. It gets worse with **assigned IDs**, like a natural key you set yourself: the entity really is new but the ID isn't null, so Hibernate merges, runs a pointless `SELECT` that finds nothing, and then inserts anyway.\n\nThe fix is to implement `Persistable<T>` and override `isNew()` so the entity answers the question directly instead of letting Spring infer it from the ID.",

  "When do you need `flush` before a subsequent query in the same transaction?":
    "Usually you don't. Hibernate queues writes and, under the default `FlushMode.AUTO`, flushes them automatically before running a JPQL query that might read the affected tables — so the ordinary save-then-query case is handled for you. The cases that need a manual `flush()` are narrower. You need it when you want a **database-generated ID** before the transaction commits, because until the INSERT actually runs there's no ID to read. You need it when you want a **constraint violation to surface at a point you control** — without a flush the exception arrives at commit, outside your try/catch and outside the method that caused it. And you need it before a **native query**, because auto-flush only reasons about JPQL: Hibernate doesn't parse your raw SQL, doesn't know which tables it touches, and won't flush pending changes before it runs. Keep one distinction straight here. `flush()` pushes your pending changes **out** to the DB; it does nothing to refresh what's already loaded. After a `@Modifying` bulk update the entities in your persistence context are stale, and flushing won't fix that — you need `clear()`, which is exactly why the annotation attribute is called `clearAutomatically`.",

  // ===================== Q94: Optimistic vs Pessimistic locking =====================
  "How does `@Version` implement optimistic locking?":
    "You add a `@Version Long version;` field and Hibernate does the rest. Every UPDATE it generates carries `WHERE id = ? AND version = ?`, using the version you originally read. If someone else committed first they've already bumped it, so your UPDATE matches **0 rows** — and a row count of zero is how Hibernate knows it lost, which is when it throws `OptimisticLockException`. Nothing blocks at the database level; both transactions run at full speed and only the first writer wins. Hibernate increments the field itself on each successful update, so you never assign it. One limit is worth stating, because it's easy to assume more protection than you have. `@Version` guards **one row**, and only against writes that go through Hibernate's dirty checking. A native `UPDATE`, or a JPQL bulk update via `@Modifying`, doesn't touch the version column — so it slips past the check entirely unless you bump `version` yourself in the statement.",

  "How should the API respond when an optimistic lock fails?":
    "JPA defines **`OptimisticLockException`**, and Spring translates it into **`ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException`** in its DAO exception hierarchy — that second one is what you'll actually catch in a Spring app. Map it to **HTTP 409 Conflict**, not a 500. The distinction matters because the status code is the instruction: a 500 tells the client the server is broken and there's nothing to do, while a 409 tells it the request collided with someone else's and is worth retrying after a refresh. If you want to retry server-side, `@Retryable(retryFor = ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException.class)` from Spring Retry handles the loop. The essential part is that the retry must **re-read the entity** first. Retrying with the same in-memory object means resubmitting the same stale version number, which fails identically every attempt until you run out of them.",

  "What does a pessimistic lock cost you in production?":
    "Deadlocks and blocked threads, and both are worse than what they replace if you don't plan for them.\n\nYou'd reach for one when contention is high enough that retrying costs more than waiting — under optimistic locking a genuinely contended row means most writers do their work and then throw it away. In Spring Data you ask for it on the repository method: `@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)` above a `findById` makes Hibernate issue `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` and hold the row until your transaction commits.\n\nThe deadlock comes from ordering. Two transactions that lock the same rows in **opposite order** wait on each other until the database kills one, so lock in a consistent order and keep the transaction short. The second cost is that a blocked thread waits forever by default — set `jakarta.persistence.lock.timeout` so it fails fast instead, or you've swapped a retryable exception for a hung request holding a connection, which is the worse trade.",

  // ===================== Q95: @Transactional =====================
  "What is the default rollback policy for runtime vs checked exceptions?":
    "The default is rollback on anything **unchecked** — `RuntimeException` and `Error` — and **commit** on checked exceptions. That second half surprises people every time. Throw a checked `IOException` out of a half-finished service method and Spring commits everything you'd written up to that point. Override it with `rollbackFor = IOException.class`, or go the other way with `noRollbackFor = BusinessValidationException.class` when an exception is really just control flow. Two related behaviours are worth knowing because they explain most \"why didn't it roll back\" confusion. If you **catch the exception inside** the transactional method, nothing rolls back — the proxy only reacts to what escapes the method, and a swallowed exception never reaches it. And the reverse case: once an inner transaction has marked the transaction rollback-only, catching that exception further out doesn't rescue anything. The commit still fails, and you get `UnexpectedRollbackException` from a method that looked like it handled the error cleanly.",

  "A `@Transactional` method called from the same class starts no transaction. How do you fix it?":
    "Move the method onto a **different bean**, so the call leaves the object and comes back through the proxy. That's the fix, and the reason is what the proxy actually is.\n\nSpring doesn't modify your class — it wraps it in a **separate object**, and only that wrapper carries the transaction advice. What gets injected into other beans is the wrapper, so calls from outside work. A `this.save()` call is a plain method call on the target itself: it never crosses the proxy boundary, no advice runs, no transaction starts. Private methods fail for the same structural reason, since a CGLIB proxy works by subclassing and can't override a private method. Nothing warns you in either case; the annotation just does nothing.\n\nSelf-injection also works — `@Lazy @Autowired private OrderService self;` then `self.save()` — and it's worth recognising, though it reads like a workaround because it is one. AspectJ weaving solves it properly by putting the advice in the bytecode, at the cost of build setup most teams don't want.",

  "Repositories are already transactional — so why put `@Transactional` on the service?":
    "Because each repository call is its own transaction. Every Spring Data method opens one if none is running, so a service that calls `save()` twice without its own `@Transactional` gets two independent transactions — and the first commit survives the second failure. The service-level annotation is what makes the pair atomic.\n\nThat's why the boundary belongs at the **service layer**: it's the only layer where one method equals one unit of work. A controller boundary fails the other way, and it's the same disease as OSIV — the transaction stays open for the whole request, including while Jackson serialises the response, so you hold a database connection during work that has nothing to do with the database.\n\nThe rule I'd give for length is that a transaction should never **span a call to another service**. The moment you're waiting on an HTTP request or a queue inside one, you're holding a pooled connection for however long that takes, and a slow dependency turns into pool exhaustion in your app.",

  // ===================== Q96: Transaction propagation =====================
  "What happens with `NOT_SUPPORTED` and `MANDATORY`?":
    "**`NOT_SUPPORTED`** suspends any active transaction and runs the method without one. The useful case is a long read you don't want holding a transaction open — a report query that takes eight seconds shouldn't be pinning a transaction the whole time, and it doesn't need one anyway. It's also how you keep something like an analytics write from being dragged into a business transaction's rollback. **`MANDATORY`** is the opposite: it refuses to run *without* a caller's transaction and throws `IllegalTransactionStateException` if there isn't one. Think of it as a compile-time-ish contract for a method that's only correct as part of a larger unit of work — instead of silently committing on its own when someone calls it from the wrong place, it fails loudly. For completeness, the seven include two more you'll almost never write: **`SUPPORTS`** joins a transaction if one exists and otherwise runs without, and **`NEVER`** throws if one exists at all. `REQUIRED` and `REQUIRES_NEW` still cover the overwhelming majority of real code.",

  "What can go wrong with `REQUIRES_NEW` when the connection pool is small?":
    "You deadlock the pool. `REQUIRES_NEW` **suspends** the outer transaction but doesn't release its connection, then asks the pool for a second one — so every thread inside that call holds **two connections at once**. With HikariCP's default `maximum-pool-size=10`, ten concurrent requests reaching the `REQUIRES_NEW` call hold all ten connections as suspended outers, and all ten inner transactions queue for a connection that can never be freed. You get `Connection is not available, request timed out after 30000ms` under load and nothing at all in testing.\n\nThe fix is either sizing the pool for the real nesting depth (roughly `threads x connections-per-thread`) or not nesting at all — publish an event and let a `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` do the independent write on its own. Trade-off: if the DB connection drops mid-audit, the outer transaction might succeed and the audit fail — log to a separate store (Kafka, dedicated audit DB) for true reliability.",

  // ===================== Q97: Isolation levels =====================
  "Explain dirty read, non-repeatable read, and phantom read.":
    "Easiest with one running example: your transaction is totalling today's orders while someone else is placing them.\n\nA **dirty read** is seeing their order **before they commit**. You add £500 to the total, their payment fails, they roll back — and you've counted money from an order that never existed. Only `READ_UNCOMMITTED` allows this, which is why nobody uses it.\n\nA **non-repeatable read** is about a row you already read. You read order 42 at £100, they update it to £150 and commit, you read order 42 again in the same transaction and now it's £150. Same row, same transaction, two different answers — so any total you computed from the first read is already wrong. `REPEATABLE_READ` is what stops this.\n\nA **phantom read** is about the set of rows rather than their values. You count today's orders and get 50, they insert a new one and commit, you run the identical query again and get 51. No row you read changed; a row **appeared**. That's what `SERIALIZABLE` prevents, and it's the most expensive to prevent because the database has to lock a range that doesn't exist yet.",

  "Does Spring choose an isolation level for you?":
    "The honest answer is that Spring doesn't pick one. `@Transactional` defaults to **`Isolation.DEFAULT`**, which means it sets nothing and takes whatever the connection already has — so your effective isolation is a property of the database, not your code. Postgres, Oracle and SQL Server give you `READ_COMMITTED`. MySQL under InnoDB gives you `REPEATABLE_READ`. That difference is the part worth carrying into an interview, because it means **the same code behaves differently on different databases**. Read a row twice in one transaction and MySQL guarantees you the same value while Postgres doesn't — so a non-repeatable read is impossible locally on MySQL and entirely possible in production on Postgres, with nothing in the source to hint at it. It also means testing against H2 tells you very little about the isolation you'll actually run under. `READ_COMMITTED` is the right place to be for a web app regardless: it rules out dirty reads and keeps concurrency high. Just know that you inherited it rather than chose it, and set the level explicitly on the one or two methods where the anomaly genuinely matters.",

  "How do you set isolation on `@Transactional`?":
    "You pass the `isolation` attribute — `@Transactional(isolation = Isolation.READ_COMMITTED)`. Spring turns that into a JDBC `Connection.setTransactionIsolation(...)` call on the connection. One catch is support: not every database and driver handles every level, and asking for `SERIALIZABLE` where it isn't supported either throws or quietly falls back. The bigger trap is that isolation belongs to the **physical transaction**, not the method. So if an inner `REQUIRED` method asks for `SERIALIZABLE` and joins an outer `READ_COMMITTED` transaction, the inner setting is **silently ignored** and you never find out. Spring only complains if you turn on `validateExistingTransaction` on the transaction manager, and that's off by default. When you genuinely need a different level, use `REQUIRES_NEW` so you get a separate physical transaction with its own connection.",

  // ===================== Q98: Migrations =====================
  "What does `ddl-auto=update` do when you rename a field?":
    "It adds a new column and leaves the old one sitting there holding your only copy of the data. `update` infers the change from your entities instead of being told, and it only ever adds — so renaming a field from `email` to `emailAddress` gets you an empty `email_address` alongside a populated `email`. No error, no rename, no backfill.\n\nNobody reviewed it and nothing recorded it, so the schema you end up with depends on the order environments happened to be deployed in. With Flyway the change is a file in the repo that someone approved in a pull request, and the script that ran on production is byte-for-byte the one you can read.\n\nWorth knowing they aren't either/or: run Flyway for the changes and set **`ddl-auto=validate`**, which applies nothing but refuses to start if your entities and the actual schema have drifted apart. That's a genuinely useful safety net. `update` is the one to keep off anything shared.",

  "Someone edited a migration that already ran and the app won't start. What now?":
    "Flyway is refusing to start because the file's contents no longer match what it recorded when the migration ran. Every applied migration gets a row in **`flyway_schema_history`** holding its version and a **CRC32 checksum**, and Flyway re-checks all of them on every startup. That's the guard against someone quietly rewriting history so two environments end up built from different SQL.\n\nIf the edit was a mistake, restore the file and the checksum matches again. If it was legitimate — you tweaked a migration on a branch nobody else has run — **`flyway repair`** rewrites the stored checksums to match the files, instead of making you edit the history table by hand.\n\nThe naming rule behind all this is `V{version}__{description}.sql`, with a **double** underscore, which is the detail everyone gets wrong first time. There's also `R__description.sql` for repeatable migrations, which deliberately re-run whenever their checksum changes — the right home for views and stored procedures, where you want the current definition rather than a chain of edits.",

  "How do you handle a failed migration in a shared environment?":
    "How bad this is depends entirely on whether your database has **transactional DDL**. On PostgreSQL it does, so a failed migration rolls back whole — the schema is untouched, Flyway records the failure, and you fix the script and redeploy. That's the easy case and it's most of them. MySQL is the hard case, because DDL there is **auto-commit**: a script that creates a table, then fails on an index, leaves the table behind. Flyway marks the migration `FAILED` and won't go further, so you have to inspect the schema, manually undo or complete what the script half-did, then clear the failed row with **`flyway repair`** before re-running. The prevention is mostly about size and shape. Keep each migration small enough that a partial application is obvious. Run it against a restored copy of production first, where you find out about the 40-minute table lock before your users do. And make schema changes **backward-compatible** so the old code still runs against the new schema — that's the expand-contract pattern: add the new column, deploy code that writes both, backfill the old rows, and only drop the old column in a later release once nothing reads it. Each step is independently safe to roll back, which is what makes zero-downtime deploys possible.",

  // ===================== Q99: Connection pooling =====================
  "Which HikariCP settings actually matter in production?":
    "Four, and really only the first one is interesting. **`maximumPoolSize`** (default **10**) caps how many connections exist at all. The instinct is to raise it under load, and that's usually wrong — a **smaller pool is often faster**, because past a point you're just adding context switches and lock contention on the database side. Hikari's own guidance lands near `cores × 2`, so a small double-digit number is normal and a pool of 100 is a red flag, not a tuning. Your database's own connection limit divided across app instances is a ceiling you mustn't exceed, not a target to aim at. **`connectionTimeout`** (default **30s**) is how long a thread waits before giving up — worth lowering on a latency-sensitive API so requests fail fast instead of piling up. **`maxLifetime`** (default **30 min**) retires connections before the database or a firewall kills them behind your back; keep it comfortably under the DB's own `wait_timeout` or you'll hand out connections that are already dead. **`idleTimeout`** just trims connections the pool isn't using, and rarely needs touching.",

  "What symptoms indicate pool exhaustion?":
    "The signature line is `HikariPool-1 - Connection is not available, request timed out after 30000ms`. Alongside it you get latency climbing across endpoints that have nothing in common except touching the database, and a thread dump full of threads parked in `HikariDataSource.getConnection()`. The thing to hold onto is that pool exhaustion is nearly always a **symptom, not the cause**. The pool is just where the pain becomes visible; something upstream is holding connections longer than it should. Usually that's a slow query keeping a transaction open for seconds, or a transaction that spans an HTTP call to another service, or **OSIV** — `spring.jpa.open-in-view` is on by default and holds a connection for the entire request including response serialisation. So the wrong fix is raising `maximumPoolSize` until the error stops. That buys a little headroom and hides the actual problem, and a bigger pool of slow connections just moves the queue into the database. Find what's holding them: check the slow query log, and check how long your transactions are open rather than how many there are.",

  "How do you monitor pool metrics with Actuator?":
    "Add `spring-boot-starter-actuator` and the HikariCP metrics register through Micrometer on their own — `hikaricp.connections.active`, `.idle`, `.pending` and `.timeout.total`. You can read any of them straight off `/actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.active`, or scrape them into Prometheus and chart them. Which one you alert on matters more than wiring it up. **`pending`** is the metric that earns its place: it counts threads currently waiting for a connection, and above zero for any sustained period means you're queueing. That's the real onset, and it moves well before anything times out. Active connections are a weaker signal than they look — sitting at maximum isn't automatically bad, it can just mean the pool is fully used by a healthy workload, so alerting on utilisation alone generates noise. And **`timeout.total`** climbing isn't an early warning at all; by then requests are already failing, so treat it as a page rather than a heads-up.",

  // ===================== Q100: SQL vs NoSQL =====================
  "Variable schema is the usual reason to reach for Mongo — does `JSONB` settle it?":
    "The usual answer is variable schema — a product catalogue where every category has different attributes, or events whose payload differs per type. That answer is weaker than it used to be, and it's worth knowing why. Postgres has **`JSONB`**: a column that stores real documents, indexes them with GIN, and lets you query inside them. So you can keep the variable part as `JSONB` and the relational part as columns, in one database, with ordinary foreign keys and transactions around both. For most teams already running Postgres that beats introducing a second datastore. What genuinely favours Mongo is the **operational** side rather than the shape of the data — sharding built in, horizontal write scale across nodes, no single primary to outgrow. If you're ingesting at a volume one machine can't take, that's real. Postgres remains the answer when the data is relational and you want joins, referential integrity, and multi-table transactions — a user-order-payment domain is not a document problem. So: Postgres by default, `JSONB` when the schema wobbles, Mongo when the write volume or distribution genuinely demands it.",

  "How do transactions differ in document stores vs relational DBs?":
    "A relational database gives you **multi-table, multi-row ACID transactions** as the baseline — it's the default assumption, not a feature you reach for. MongoDB's baseline is narrower: **single-document atomicity**. Every field update within one document lands together, and anything spanning two documents historically didn't. **v4.0** added multi-document transactions, but they carry real overhead and they need a **replica set** — they don't work against a standalone, which is why people often find them unavailable the first time they try. The conclusion is a modelling one rather than a feature comparison. If you need to atomically update two collections, that's usually a sign the documents are drawn along the wrong boundary. Mongo's model expects related data to live **inside** one document, so the order and its line items are one document you update atomically, not two collections you coordinate. Design that way and cross-document transactions stop being something you need. Keep needing them and you're fighting the database rather than using it.",

  "Can you use both SQL and NoSQL in one Spring Boot system?":
    "Yes — it's called **polyglot persistence**, and mechanically it's easy. Put `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` and `spring-boot-starter-data-mongodb` on the classpath, configure each datasource, and `JpaRepository` and `MongoRepository` beans coexist happily. The usual shape is Postgres for core transactional data, plus Redis for cache and sessions. The trap is that **`@Transactional` doesn't span both**. A method that saves to Postgres and then writes to Mongo is doing two independent operations, and the annotation only governs the JPA half — if the Mongo write fails, the Postgres transaction has already committed or will commit regardless, and nothing rolls back for you. You need compensating writes, an outbox, or a design where the second store is allowed to lag. Worth being honest about the cost too. Two datastores means two backup and restore procedures, two failure modes, two things to be on call for. Redis alongside Postgres is routine because the cache is disposable. A **second primary datastore** — one holding data you can't lose — should need a reason you can state in one sentence, and \"the schema is a bit variable\" isn't it.",

  // ===================== Q101: Indexing =====================
  "Which indexes should you drop?":
    "**`pg_stat_user_indexes`** gives you `idx_scan` per index, and anything still at zero after a full business cycle is pure write tax you can drop. That's the whole job: index what you filter, join and sort on, then go looking for the ones nobody uses.\n\nThe tax is real and it's called **write amplification** — one logical row change turns into many physical writes, because the row goes into the table and then again into every index covering it. An INSERT or DELETE touches all of them, so ten indexes make a single insert eleven separate B-tree updates.\n\nAn UPDATE is a bit kinder, only touching indexes on the **columns that actually changed** — though on Postgres an update that can't be done in place rewrites all of them anyway. On a high-volume table like payment events that's the difference between comfortable and I/O-bound, and the indexes cost storage and `VACUUM` time on top.",

  "What is a composite index, and does column order matter?":
    "A **composite index** covers multiple columns — `CREATE INDEX idx_orders ON orders(user_id, status, created_at)`. Column order **absolutely matters**: a query filtering only on `user_id` can use this index (leftmost prefix), a query on `user_id AND status` can use it. But a query filtering **only on `status`** cannot use it (no leftmost prefix match). Think of it like a phone book sorted by last name then first name — you can look up \"Smith\" or \"Smith, John\" but not \"John\" alone. Order the columns by how they're used rather than by cardinality: **equality filters first, then range conditions, then sort columns**. For `WHERE user_id = ? AND created_at > ?` that means `(user_id, created_at)` — putting the higher-cardinality `created_at` first would be actively worse, because a range condition stops the index being usable for anything after it.",

  "How would you find out that a slow query is missing an index?":
    "Start with `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on the query — and note the `ANALYZE` part matters, because plain `EXPLAIN` only shows the planner's estimates while `ANALYZE` actually runs it and reports real times and row counts. What you're looking for is a **Seq Scan on a large table with a selective filter**, alongside a big `rows removed by filter` number, which is the database saying it read everything and threw nearly all of it away. MySQL's `EXPLAIN` says the same thing as `type = ALL` with an empty `key`. A sequential scan on its own isn't a bug, though, and this is where people waste effort — on a small table a `Seq Scan` is genuinely faster than an index lookup and the planner picks it deliberately. Adding an index to a 500-row table changes nothing. Also compare estimated against actual rows: if they diverge wildly the problem may be **stale statistics** rather than a missing index, and the fix is `ANALYZE`, not `CREATE INDEX`. To find candidates in the first place, turn on the slow query log with `log_min_duration_statement = 1000` and use **`pg_stat_statements`** to rank by total time — total, not per-call, since a 20ms query run a million times hurts more than a 2-second report. And check the plan against production-sized data, because the planner's choices depend on table statistics and a query that uses an index on 500 local rows may not on 50 million.",

  // ===================== Q102: JOINs =====================
  "Why does a `WHERE` on the right-hand table turn a LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN?":
    "Because the `NULL`s it just filled in can't satisfy your condition. `WHERE o.status = 'PAID'` evaluates `NULL = 'PAID'` for every unmatched row, which isn't true, so all of them get filtered straight back out — leaving exactly the rows an INNER JOIN would have given you.\n\nConditions on the right-hand table belong in the **`ON`** clause, where they're part of matching. Only conditions on the left table belong in `WHERE`.\n\nThe behaviour underneath is that a LEFT JOIN promises **every row from the left table**, filling right-hand columns with `NULL` when the `ON` finds nothing. `SELECT u.name, o.total FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id` returns all users, with `o.total` null for anyone who's never ordered — which is also the idiom for finding absence, since `WHERE o.id IS NULL` gives you exactly the users with no orders.",

  "How do joins relate to JPA association fetching?":
    "They're the same thing seen from two levels. `FetchType.EAGER` on a `@ManyToOne`, or a `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL, both come out of Hibernate as a real SQL `JOIN` — one query carrying parent and child together. Load lazily instead and Hibernate issues a separate `SELECT` each time you touch the association, which is N+1 described in SQL terms. The distinction that actually costs people data is inner versus left. **`JOIN FETCH` is an inner join**, so it drops any parent row whose association is empty. `SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.coupon` looks like it returns orders, but every order **without** a coupon silently disappears from the result — you asked for orders and got only the couponed ones. On a nullable association or an empty collection that's a data-loss bug rather than a performance detail, and it's why you write **`LEFT JOIN FETCH`** by default and only use the inner form when you genuinely want to filter to parents that have one.",

  "What is a CROSS JOIN, and when is it accidental/bad?":
    "A **CROSS JOIN** is the Cartesian product — every row of A paired with every row of B. Two tables of 1,000 rows produce 1,000,000 rows, so the size grows by multiplication rather than addition, which is what makes it dangerous at scale. Accidentally you get one by omitting the `ON` clause, or in JPQL by naming two unrelated entities in the `FROM` — `FROM User u, Order o` with no join condition is a cross join wearing ordinary clothes. On two large tables that saturates the database, floods the network buffers, and OOMs the JVM trying to materialise the result. It does have honest uses, though, and knowing them is what makes you sure about the accidental case. Report grids are the usual one: cross a list of accounts against a generated date series so every account has a row for every day, then left-join the actual activity onto it — that's how you get a report where days with no transactions still show up as zero instead of missing entirely. Deliberate and against a small generated set, it's a normal tool. The problem is only ever the one you didn't mean to write.",
};
